WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.160
All right.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.150
Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:09.650
I am Isabel Zimmerman.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:13.900
I am here to demystify
machine learning operations,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.970
mostly because machine learning
operations are very hard, so

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.520
infamously hard
that in a past life

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.900
I was deploying a lot of models.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.370
And I found these tools so
unfriendly for data scientists

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:30.580
that I left that job, came to
the company previously known

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.910
as RStudio, to build
tools for data scientists

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.170
to deploy their models.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.470
But maybe more important
than this fact is,

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:42.110
I really love baking.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:43.440
I love baking.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.060
I love eating chocolate
chips by the handful.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.110
I find them to be so
delicious, especially

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.500
the dark chocolate, chunks.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.140
Those are my favorite.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.690
And as many chocolate
chips as I eat,

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.420
it's never as good as the
cookie at the end of my baking

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.700
session.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.720
And machine learning models
are a lot like chocolate chips.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.700
They're really
good on their own,

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.020
but they're never as
good as they could be.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.905
Or a lot of the
value you're losing

00:01:07.905 --> 00:01:09.280
when they're not
deployed as part

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.560
of some sort of larger system.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.380
So what is MLOps?

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:17.960
It's a set of practices to
deploy and maintain machine

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.590
learning models in production
reliably and efficiently.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:24.950
It's a set of guidelines to
help your model live outside

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:26.330
of a notebook.

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:29.300
So what are some MLOps practices?

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:30.770
If we were in the
kitchen together,

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.020
I'd tell you to write
down your recipe

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.120
and to remember to put
your cookies in the oven

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.590
and don't just eat
the cookie dough

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:40.480
and make sure that you don't
burn your cookies at the end.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.270
But in the data science world,
this looks a little different.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.580
Some practices that you
might want to keep in mind

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.520
are to version your model,
to deploy your model

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.170
and to monitor your model.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:52.700
If you were at the
keynote earlier,

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.830
this might look pretty
familiar, except for I

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:59.200
like to think my version
is a little more delicious.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.070
So, it's important to
realize that MLPs is not

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:06.400
a disjoint piece of this
data science lifecycle,

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.340
that you build your model and
kind of throw it off to your IT

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.050
folks and give them a thumbs up
and tell them to deal with it.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:14.450
No, this is our job too.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:17.410
We collect the data we
understand and clean

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:21.040
our data with fantastic tools
like the tidyverse or data

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:21.820
table.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:25.810
If you're in R, if you use
Python, like I secretly do,

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:29.830
you might use something like
Siuba or Pandas or NumPy.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:32.590
Once you've cleaned and
understood your data,

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.370
you it's time to train
and evaluate your model.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:37.570
Once again, we have
great tools for this,

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:41.660
like tidymodels and torch
and caret and tidymodels.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:45.280
And on the Python side,
we have scikit-learn,

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.740
torch, xgboost, great tools, but
then it gets a little hazy.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.550
What what comes next?

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:54.850
And that's where
vetiver steps in.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:58.330
So if whatever is offered
in both Python and in R,

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:00.520
which means you can
pip install vetiver

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.490
or install.packages
vetiver and it

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:06.400
helps you do these things
of versioning, deploying

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.470
and monitoring a model.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.150
So we'll start with versioning.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.730
How do we track
and manage change?

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.970
When I bake, I might try out
a little bit of a new recipe,

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:19.830
like throw in some gluten
free flour or oops, like

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.850
bought the wrong ingredients,
going to have to make it work

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:24.740
and I'll write down
my changes so I

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:26.750
can reproduce this model later.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:28.580
But in the data
science world, it

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.280
looks more like
you saved a model

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.350
and you do your
training and your tuning

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:36.290
and all of this other fun stuff
that we've heard so much about.

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:39.500
And you end up with
your final model.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.270
And then you actually
get some new data

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:43.190
or you realize you
did something wrong

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:47.140
and then you have your
actual final model.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:50.200
But this is not over, because
models are living objects

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.130
and there's still more to do.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:55.510
And now we have our
actual final model.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:58.610
So this lacks context
and it's not scalable.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:01.390
We can see it gets kind
of crazy for one model.

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:07.080
So what happens when you
have 5 or 10 or 50 or 100?

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.500
Versioning is useful to
track changes across time

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.860
and it should also be used
for different implementations.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:17.230
So we saw that versioning
is useful across time.

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:22.110
So you're not doing the
model one, two, three, 17

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:26.860
but it's also important if you
have different implementations.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:28.690
So if you have a staging server,

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:32.040
and a production server, if
you have multiple models,

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.860
being able to have strong
versioning procedures

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.790
makes you able to take them down
and put them up or roll back

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.100
to old versions very easily.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.190
It's important to have
a strong structure

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:45.990
around your
versioning procedures

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.150
really any time you
have multiple models,

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.210
this is important.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:54.800
So in vetiver, we actually have
a secret weapon called pins.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:00.340
And pins is once again
available in R and Python.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:03.840
So you'll load this package and
you start with a model board.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.340
So boards hold, they
organize, and they

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:09.550
can create some metadata
for almost any object.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:13.120
And when you use it in
conjunction with vetiver,

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.292
you get some special features.

00:05:16.292 --> 00:05:18.500
But before you get to look
at those special features,

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.700
you also have to make
a vetiver model.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:23.760
So this is a deployable
model object.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.420
You make a vetiver
model from your model,

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:29.660
and you can give it some other
information like the name,

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:34.050
for example name or maybe
an input data prototype.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.380
So we're giving it a little
bit of training data.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:38.360
So when you deploy
your model, you

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:42.180
know what to expect
from your incoming data.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.340
So we have our model
board, model board,

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.400
our vetiver model, v,
and then we can vetiver

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.460
pin write v to our board

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:52.470
and your model is versioned.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.390
And it's also really
cool because this can

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.350
be on many different platforms.

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:59.190
You can make a board,
maybe AWS

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:03.720
or on RStudio Connect, and so
you can collaborate and bring

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.400
these models into
memory, even when

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.760
your teammate is pushing
them up into your board

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.900
on a different server.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.410
So this helps with scale.

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:15.020
But what about context?

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.320
So if we want to look
into our model named "name",

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.770
we can see some very
descriptive information

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:27.410
on what type of model we created
the size, the hash, a title,

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:28.890
the type of model.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:32.300
And also we can peek in
to that data prototype

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:35.150
to see what this
model is expecting,

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:39.680
as well as how to run the
model with required packages.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:42.230
So we have some infrastructure
to version our model

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:44.630
and now it is time to deploy.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:47.660
So when building out vetiver,
there's many different ways

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:52.550
you can deploy a model, but
not all ways to deploy a model

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.580
are made equally.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:59.030
So we'll start with a
few different flavors.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:04.270
And one way you can deploy
a model is an XML with PMML.

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:06.280
And if I was baking,
I would tell you

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:09.070
that this is a lot like
baking on an open flame.

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:11.950
Fires are notoriously
very portable,

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:15.040
but they're not very
customizable in temperature.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:18.010
So while XML can be
put literally anywhere

00:07:18.010 --> 00:07:21.910
on the internet, PMML only gives
you a few different options

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:22.420
for models.

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:23.920
And we've seen
today, like there are

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:26.630
so many amazing ways
to build a model.

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.390
We don't want to
restrict data scientists.

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:32.460
So we knew XML was
not the right place.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:36.560
We also peeked into
deploying into databases.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:39.520
So this is kind of like baking
cookies in a waffle iron.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:42.980
It works really well for
some recipes, but others,

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:47.960
you kind of end up with this
weird half baked goopy mess.

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:51.500
So deploying in databases
is great for people

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:55.550
who have a workflow
based around a database

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:58.700
and you have easy access to it.

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:01.730
But this isn't the best case
scenario for every team,

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:04.430
and we wanted to give
as many people an option

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:06.937
to deploy their
models as possible.

00:08:06.937 --> 00:08:09.020
So if you're a baker,
you're probably not carrying

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:11.360
around matches and you're
probably not carrying

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:12.880
around a waffle iron either.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.220
You're probably best
friends with your oven.

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.860
Best known for its
predictable temperatures

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:22.340
and easy to use interface to
make baking a great experience

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:24.200
for all skill levels.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:26.360
REST APIs are the same way.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:29.120
They are interfaces that
connect to many different types

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:32.450
of applications in a
very standardized way.

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:36.890
Any model that you can write,
you can deploy into a REST API.

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:40.140
You can interact in the
browser to debug your API.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.830
You can deploy it
and run it locally

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:47.790
on your computer or an on
prem server or AWS SageMaker.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:49.910
And if you're interested
in AWS SageMaker,

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:51.710
there is a birds of
a feather session

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:54.630
about vetiver and SageMaker.

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:58.020
Or maybe you're interested
in deploying into Connect.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:01.520
And there is another
talk called "Yes, you

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:03.950
can use Python with
RStudio Team" that

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:08.540
can show you vetiver inside this
amazing integrated application

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:12.770
that's all deployed on Connect
that those are both tomorrow I

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:13.680
think afternoon.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.740
But check your schedules and
don't quote me right now.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:17.240
All right.

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:20.150
We have one last reason
to be excited about APIs.

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:23.090
They are really accessible
for all skill levels.

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:26.050
So maybe your teammate
that isn't as modeling

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.040
obsessed as present company.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:31.390
You're still able, they're still
able to discover and explore

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:32.960
and interact with your model.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:36.520
And they don't even have
to download R or Python.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:38.040
This is a lot of hype.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:41.750
So how do we even
deploy this model?

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:42.930
In the R side.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.050
You start up your
Plumber router.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:49.600
Create a vetiver API and our "v"
vetiver model from earlier,

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:51.720
just plug it in.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:55.020
On the Python side, once
again create a vetiver model

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:59.340
and create a vetiver API
and plug-in your model, v.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:06.420
And once you have run
that you get prompted

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:09.780
with this great visual
documentation that

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:11.570
lives at your API.

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:13.320
It will give you a
little bit of metadata,

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:15.810
like name, the
version of vetiver

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.730
you're deploying with, kind
of what model you're using,

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:20.500
as well as where
this API is running.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:23.410
So you can see this
is a local server.

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:27.100
But if you scroll down,
there is also a ping endpoint

00:10:27.100 --> 00:10:29.060
to see if your model
is up and running.

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:30.850
If you keep scrolling
down, you can

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:33.490
see there's a predict endpoint
where you can actually

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:35.130
interact with this model.

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:37.370
So we see I'm
making a prediction.

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:42.640
And then I can edit some things
and rerun it in the browser.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:45.190
And I was typing slower
than I'm talking right now,

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:48.410
but we can try and voila
our prediction changed.

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:50.390
We can see response
headers and curl

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:53.250
request information to
make your IT people happy.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:57.497
But if you're not
interested in looking

00:10:57.497 --> 00:10:59.080
at this endpoint too
much and you just

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:01.900
want to interact with the model
that somebody else has deployed

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:05.080
or that you have
deployed, you can also

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:08.200
just live inside the notebook
that you're already inside.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:12.350
So you will use predict just like all
of these tidy models packages.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:15.100
So it's an expected function.

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:17.150
Give them where your
model is running at.

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:19.350
So this vetiver
endpoint and the data

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:20.980
that you'd like to
predict with here,

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:25.120
we can see I'm doing batch
predictions on mtcars so you

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:28.180
don't have to leave your
computational environment

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:30.850
if you don't want to.

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:35.140
So, we versioned our model,
we've deployed our model

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:37.780
and our cookies are
baking in our API oven,

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:42.108
but we have to keep an eye
on them so they don't burn.

00:11:42.108 --> 00:11:44.150
It's important to monitor
a few different things.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:45.840
The first is data drift.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:51.140
So does your data look the same
today as it did two months ago?

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:54.380
You also want to
monitor for model drift.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:58.010
So model drift is when your
model's performance metrics

00:11:58.010 --> 00:11:59.220
start to decay.

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:03.050
And this is so, so
important to track.

00:12:03.050 --> 00:12:07.460
Models fail silently, so it will
continue running without error,

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:12.430
even if your model
accuracy is zero.

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:15.640
If you're not monitoring
your model in some way,

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:20.390
you are oblivious to
model decay in production.

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:23.930
A good example of this is
I listened to way more Jonas

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:28.460
Brothers in 2012 than I do
now, but my Spotify algorithm

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:31.700
doesn't still suggest that
I listen to Jonas Brothers

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:33.890
because my tastes have adapted.

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:37.760
If this recommendation
algorithm hadn't kept up

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:42.930
with my changing choices, they
would have lost a customer.

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:45.540
Finally, it's important
to know what to do

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:47.340
when things are going wrong.

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:49.740
So if your model
is declining, it

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:51.550
might be retraining
with new data.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:53.620
And this works in
a lot of use cases.

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:56.070
You might need to try out a
new model type altogether,

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:59.180
but it all goes back
to this versioning.

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:01.470
If you have strongly
versioned models

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:05.790
and your API is connected
to this version,

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:10.170
it makes it so much easier to
take down and put up new models

00:13:10.170 --> 00:13:13.000
without much pain on your end.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:17.240
So in vetiver, we are going
to start by computing metrics.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:19.788
So this is coming
from a data frame that

00:13:19.788 --> 00:13:21.580
has a few different
columns and we're going

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:23.180
to pass it into this function.

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:25.490
So we want to give
them the date column.

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:27.790
So this is the date that
the prediction was made.

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:30.640
We also want to
give the time frame

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:32.360
that we're aggregating over.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.600
So for this one, we're
looking at one week.

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:36.100
So maybe this model
has been running

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:37.810
for years and years
and years, but we

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:40.030
want to look at it
one week at a time.

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:44.920
Then we want to give the actual
miles per gallon or the truth

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:49.620
value and we want to give
the predicted value as well.

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:53.580
There's also you can send in
different metrics sets. R does

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:55.560
this secretly behind
the scenes for you

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:57.600
but you can also customize them.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.330
Python side have to
write a little bit of "I

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:04.440
want to use RMSE" and
send in a list.

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:07.340
So we've computed our metrics.

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:09.660
And then we want
to pin our metrics.

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.120
This is once again
very important

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:14.840
to have this running log
of your model performance.

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:17.360
With vetiver pin metrics
you pass in the metrics

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:18.550
you've just created.

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:22.310
You give it a name for your
pen, and if you have overlapping

00:14:22.310 --> 00:14:25.640
dates, you can choose to
overwrite them and let

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:31.140
vetiver do all the hard lifting
of dealing with date columns.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:34.040
Finally, this is the
beauty of it all.

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:38.423
You get this out of the box
function to plot your metrics.

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:45.710
So putting it all together, some
best practices for deployment

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:49.040
are versioning your model,
deploying your model

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:51.890
and monitoring your model.

00:14:51.890 --> 00:14:55.650
But there are more best
practices to keep in mind.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:58.460
One of them is to think
about model cards.

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:02.090
And these are partially
automated RMarkdown templates

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:04.210
that come inside
the vetiver package.

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:06.590
This is where you can help
other people on your team

00:15:06.590 --> 00:15:09.020
better understand your model.

00:15:09.020 --> 00:15:12.410
So you can document
environmental or technical or

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:16.070
ethical factors that you
thought so hard about when

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:18.410
you were developing your model.

00:15:18.410 --> 00:15:22.260
There's also things to
help with data validation.

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:25.850
So there is something called
an input data prototype or

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:28.550
p-type in vetiver
that will validate

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:32.920
your data at prediction
time at the API level.

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:36.340
Because sometimes the
world messes up your data

00:15:36.340 --> 00:15:38.920
and as it streams
into your API, it

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:43.000
does not look the way that your
model thought it was going to.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:46.060
And vetiver is able to give
you helpful error messages

00:15:46.060 --> 00:15:50.320
to make sure you can
choose or identify

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:53.380
these points of
failure very easily.

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:57.460
And there are many more best
practices to keep in mind,

00:15:57.460 --> 00:15:59.890
but our cookies
are taking a bow,

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:01.610
so it must be the
end of our time.

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:04.180
And I challenge you all
to think of what can you

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:06.580
do with your own data
science workflow?

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:10.390
Where is you or your team
today and where can you

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:14.230
add in ML Ops best practices
to make it a little better?

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:17.830
I also encourage you to check
out vetiver in Python or R

00:16:17.830 --> 00:16:21.190
and if you have any
questions, Julia, Silge and I

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:24.040
and the rest of the vetiver
team and tidymodels team

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:26.790
will be right outside
after my talk.

00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:27.320
Thank you.

00:16:36.527 --> 00:16:37.360
Thank you, Isabel.

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:38.850
We have time for
a few questions.

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:40.740
Yeah so you said briefly, but.

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:43.310
But how well does that
ever play with RStudio

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:45.510
or Posit Connect?

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:50.910
Well, there are really easy like
one liners where it's deploy,

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:54.170
RStudio Connect and it will
ship your model off

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:55.640
into the Connect world.

00:16:55.640 --> 00:16:57.020
Great.

00:16:57.020 --> 00:16:59.600
Can you deploy
multiple vetiver models

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:04.920
to different routes on
the same API server?

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:10.234
So it depends on
what you're doing.

00:17:10.234 --> 00:17:12.859
I guess there's a few different
ways to approach this question.

00:17:12.859 --> 00:17:15.280
One, you can have
multiple different end

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:17.400
points running at once.

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:20.040
Which might be a better
solution rather than

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:23.369
trying to hit the same
endpoint at or like same API

00:17:23.369 --> 00:17:24.609
at different endpoints.

00:17:24.609 --> 00:17:27.000
You can also write
custom end points,

00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:31.650
so maybe you want your
model at one level and then

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:36.600
some processing
or multiply by 100

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:38.710
to get a percentage or
something like that.

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:42.440
And that also is part
of vetiver, right?

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:44.780
Does vetiver work with
any Python packages

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:46.970
besides scikit-learn and torch?

00:17:46.970 --> 00:17:51.230
So currently those are
our two supported packages

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:53.580
for deploying models
out of the box.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:55.610
But you can deploy
any model because we

00:17:55.610 --> 00:17:58.980
have kind of an escape
hatch of a custom Handler.

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:01.490
So if how your model
needs to make predictions,

00:18:01.490 --> 00:18:04.870
there's documentation
on the Python side.

00:18:04.870 --> 00:18:08.660
I think it's like
advanced usage.

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:10.970
Custom handlers click on
that and it will give you

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:12.830
all the information you need.

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:14.160
One last quick question.

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:16.430
What is the back end
for deploying a REST

00:18:16.430 --> 00:18:18.000
API on the Python side?

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:19.560
That's Fast API.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:21.630
I am a big fan.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:23.280
OK Thank you, Isabel.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:25.280
Thank you all.

